From: NimRTC contributors <bot@nimrtc.local>
Subject: [PATCH] libjuice: do not share a UDP port, and do not merge
 ephemeral-port agents into one mux registry

Upstream base commit: 77daa8befd828046169adbb012d8de928bfdcdd4

Two NimRTC-local changes on top of the pinned upstream revision.  Both
replace a change that used to live here (the SO_REUSEADDR/SO_REUSEPORT
set), which is itself removed by this patch.

1. src/udp.c: drop SO_REUSEADDR and SO_REUSEPORT.

   Both make a second bind on an already-bound UDP port *succeed*, so
   two sockets end up sharing one port.  With SO_REUSEADDR set, the
   ephemeral allocator is unsafe as well: binding to port 0 can hand
   back a port a live socket already owns.  Measured on Linux with the
   ephemeral range narrowed to 11 ports: 30 sockets bound, but only 11
   distinct ports were handed out.

   A shared UDP port breaks ICE across processes: libjuice wakes its
   mux thread with a 0-byte datagram sent to its own address
   (conn_mux_interrupt -> udp_sendto_self), the kernel may deliver it
   to the *other* process's socket, and the interrupting process then
   stays in poll() for its full 60 s timeout while juice_destroy()
   blocks in thread_join().

   Nothing is lost: UDP sockets have no TIME_WAIT, so the port is
   released as soon as the socket closes.

2. src/conn_mux.c: an ephemeral-port request must not reuse an existing
   mux registry.

   conn_mux_get_registry() matched on impl->port >= config->port_begin,
   which is always true when port_begin == 0, so the first registry in
   the process absorbed every later agent.  Two agents then shared one
   UDP socket, advertised the same 127.0.0.1:port candidate, and could
   not be told apart: the STUN Binding request came back with a ufrag
   mismatch and the session never connected.
diff --git a/src/udp.c b/src/udp.c
index 993ec0f..d65d244 100644
--- a/src/udp.c
+++ b/src/udp.c
@@ -53,6 +53,49 @@ static socket_t create_socket_for_addrinfo(const udp_socket_config_t *config,
 		return INVALID_SOCKET;
 	}
 
+	// NOTE: no SO_REUSEADDR / SO_REUSEPORT here.
+	//
+	// NimRTC used to set both here (see
+	// src/third_party/libjuice/patches/0001-so-reuseaddr.patch).  Both
+	// break ICE silently, and the second one breaks it in a way that is
+	// easy to mistake for a CI flake:
+	//
+	//   - On Linux and macOS SO_REUSEPORT lets a second socket bind a
+	//     port that is already bound, and on every platform SO_REUSEADDR
+	//     does the same for UDP.  Two sockets then share one UDP port.
+	//   - Worse, SO_REUSEADDR also makes the *ephemeral* allocator unsafe:
+	//     with it set, binding to port 0 can hand back a port that a live
+	//     socket already owns.  Measured on Linux with the ephemeral
+	//     range narrowed to 11 ports: 30 sockets bound, but only 11
+	//     distinct ports were handed out -- 19 pairs shared a port.
+	//     Without SO_REUSEADDR the same 30 sockets could only bind 11
+	//     times; the rest failed with EADDRINUSE, as they should.
+	//
+	// A shared UDP port breaks ICE in two ways:
+	//
+	//   1. Across processes: libjuice wakes its mux thread with a 0-byte
+	//      datagram sent to its own address (conn_mux_interrupt ->
+	//      udp_sendto_self).  With two sockets sharing the port the
+	//      kernel may deliver that datagram to the *other* process's
+	//      socket, so the interrupting process stays in poll() for its
+	//      full 60 s timeout and juice_destroy() blocks in thread_join()
+	//      inside conn_mux_registry_cleanup.  The test then hangs until
+	//      ctest kills it -- exactly the 60.01 s timeout we saw.
+	//   2. Within one process: two agents on the same port offer the
+	//      same candidate, so they cannot be told apart by the mux
+	//      demux.
+	//
+	// Nothing is lost by not setting SO_REUSEADDR: UDP sockets have no
+	// TIME_WAIT, so the port is released the moment the socket closes
+	// (verified on both Windows and Linux).
+	//
+	// The other reason to leave the socket options alone is the Windows
+	// CI runner, which reserves whole port ranges (Hyper-V / HNS).  A
+	// bind inside one fails with WSAEACCES (errno 10013), and libjuice
+	// walks the whole requested range before giving up, so the agent
+	// fails to open outright.  Asking the kernel for an ephemeral port
+	// never lands in an excluded range.
+
 	// Listen on both IPv6 and IPv4
 	const sockopt_t disabled = 0;
 	if (ai->ai_family == AF_INET6)
diff --git a/src/conn_mux.c b/src/conn_mux.c
index 1c406ae..85458e0 100644
--- a/src/conn_mux.c
+++ b/src/conn_mux.c
@@ -57,6 +57,28 @@ static int conn_mux_registries_size;
 static int conn_mux_registries_count;
 
 conn_registry_t *conn_mux_get_registry(udp_socket_config_t *config) {
+	// An ephemeral-port request (port_begin == 0 && port_end == 0) means
+	// "give me a socket and let the kernel pick the port".  It must NOT
+	// reuse an existing registry: the caller has no way to know which
+	// port it will end up on, so merging it into somebody else's socket
+	// would silently give two agents the same local candidate.
+	//
+	// The previous code matched on `impl->port >= config->port_begin`,
+	// which is always true when port_begin == 0, so the *first* registry
+	// in the process absorbed every later agent.  Two ICE agents in one
+	// process (e.g. two NimRTCEngine instances talking to each other over
+	// the loopback) then shared one UDP socket, advertised the same
+	// 127.0.0.1:port candidate, and could not be told apart: the STUN
+	// Binding request came back with a ufrag mismatch and the session
+	// never connected.
+	//
+	// PR #49 worked around this in the tests by switching them to Poll
+	// mode.  Fixing it here means the *default* Mux mode is safe too, so
+	// an application that creates two engines does not have to know about
+	// this at all.
+	if (config->port_begin == 0 && config->port_end == 0)
+		return NULL;
+
 	for (int i = 0; i < conn_mux_registries_size; i++) {
 		if (!conn_mux_registries[i]) {
 			continue;
